Here's the recommended structure for testing your MCP server without calling the real API:

## Project Structure

```
MyMcpServer/
├── MyMcpServer/
│   ├── MyMcpServer.csproj
│   └── Tools/MyTools.cs
└── MyMcpServer.Tests/
    ├── MyMcpServer.Tests.csproj
    ├── Helpers/MockHttpMessageHandler.cs
    ├── Unit/MyToolTests.cs
    └── Integration/ServerIntegrationTests.cs
```

## Setup

```bash
dotnet new xunit -n MyMcpServer.Tests
cd MyMcpServer.Tests
dotnet add reference ../MyMcpServer/MyMcpServer.csproj
dotnet add package ModelContextProtocol
dotnet add package Moq
dotnet add package FluentAssertions
```

## Key Pattern: Mock `HttpMessageHandler`

Create a reusable fake for HTTP calls:

```csharp
public class MockHttpMessageHandler : HttpMessageHandler
{
    private readonly string _response;
    private readonly HttpStatusCode _statusCode;

    public MockHttpMessageHandler(string response, HttpStatusCode statusCode = HttpStatusCode.OK)
    {
        _response = response;
        _statusCode = statusCode;
    }

    protected override Task<HttpResponseMessage> SendAsync(
        HttpRequestMessage request, CancellationToken cancellationToken) =>
        Task.FromResult(new HttpResponseMessage
        {
            StatusCode = _statusCode,
            Content = new StringContent(_response)
        });
}
```

## Unit Tests (fast, isolated)

Test tool methods directly with the mocked HTTP:

```csharp
public class ApiToolTests
{
    [Fact]
    public async Task FetchUser_ReturnsUserData()
    {
        var handler = new MockHttpMessageHandler("""{"name": "Alice"}""");
        var httpClient = new HttpClient(handler) { BaseAddress = new Uri("https://fake.api") };

        var result = await ApiTools.FetchUser(httpClient, "user-1");

        result.Should().Contain("Alice");
    }

    [Fact]
    public async Task FetchUser_HandlesNotFound()
    {
        var handler = new MockHttpMessageHandler("", HttpStatusCode.NotFound);
        var httpClient = new HttpClient(handler) { BaseAddress = new Uri("https://fake.api") };

        var result = await ApiTools.FetchUser(httpClient, "missing");

        result.Should().Contain("not found");
    }
}
```

## Integration Tests (full MCP protocol, in-memory)

Use `ClientServerTestBase` to test through the MCP protocol with DI-injected fakes:

```csharp
public class ServerIntegrationTests : ClientServerTestBase
{
    public ServerIntegrationTests(ITestOutputHelper output) : base(output) { }

    protected override void ConfigureServices(
        ServiceCollection services, IMcpServerBuilder builder)
    {
        builder.WithTools<ApiTools>();

        // Replace the real HttpClient with a fake
        var handler = new MockHttpMessageHandler("""{"name": "Alice"}""");
        services.AddSingleton<HttpClient>(
            new HttpClient(handler) { BaseAddress = new Uri("https://fake.api") });
    }

    [Fact]
    public async Task ListTools_IncludesFetchUser()
    {
        await using var client = await CreateMcpClientForServer();
        var tools = await client.ListToolsAsync();
        tools.Should().Contain(t => t.Name == "fetch_user");
    }

    [Fact]
    public async Task FetchUser_ReturnsMockedResponse()
    {
        await using var client = await CreateMcpClientForServer();
        var result = await client.CallToolAsync("fetch_user",
            new() { ["userId"] = "user-1" });
        var text = result.Content.OfType<TextContentBlock>().First().Text;
        text.Should().Contain("Alice");
    }
}
```

## Summary

| Layer | What it tests | Speed | Mocking approach |
|-------|--------------|-------|-----------------|
| **Unit** | Tool method logic | ~ms | `MockHttpMessageHandler` passed directly |
| **Integration** | Full MCP protocol + DI | ~ms | Fake services registered in DI container |

The key insight: inject `HttpClient` via DI in your tools, then replace the `HttpMessageHandler` in tests. This gives you full coverage without any real network calls.
